1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,513
Today is October 17, 2026.

3
00:00:07,513 --> 00:00:14,878
Overnight, Wall Street endured a fierce liquidity squeeze as monthly options expiration triggered extreme volatility.

4
00:00:14,878 --> 00:00:20,589
The CBOE Volatility Index surged thirty-six percent to twenty-two point three.

5
00:00:20,589 --> 00:00:25,527
The CNN Fear and Greed index plummeted straight into extreme fear.

6
00:00:25,527 --> 00:00:32,513
At the closing bell, the S and P five hundred dropped one point zero nine percent to seventy-six twenty.

7
00:00:32,513 --> 00:00:39,437
The tech-heavy Nasdaq sank two point one four percent, slicing below its two hundred day moving average.

8
00:00:39,437 --> 00:00:45,246
In stark contrast, the Dow Jones Industrial Average bucked the trend to finish in the green.

9
00:00:45,246 --> 00:00:50,491
Defensive rotations cushioned broader carnage, but tech endured a brutal rout.

10
00:00:50,491 --> 00:00:57,329
In rates and commodities, the thirty-year Treasury yield hovered near five point six percent before pulling back.

11
00:00:57,329 --> 00:01:04,474
Meanwhile, crude prices jumped on escalating Middle East conflicts and cross-border energy infrastructure strikes.

12
00:01:04,474 --> 00:01:10,184
Congressional pressure to revoke Russian diesel waivers added fresh fuel to the oil rally.

13
00:01:10,184 --> 00:01:14,829
European bond spreads ignited additional global macro alarms.

14
00:01:14,829 --> 00:01:18,898
Ten-year French sovereign yields reached twenty-five year highs.

15
00:01:18,898 --> 00:01:24,829
Their spread over German Bunds widened to one hundred and forty basis points amid fiscal gridlock.

16
00:01:24,829 --> 00:01:31,630
On central bank policy, Federal Reserve Chair Jerome Powell spoke at the IMF meetings in Bangkok.

17
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:35,601
Powell dismantled expectations for pre-meeting guidance.

18
00:01:35,601 --> 00:01:40,368
He emphasized that the Fed will remain purely data-dependent into November.

19
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:47,672
He characterized artificial intelligence as a generational economic force driving immediate demand-side capital spending.

20
00:01:47,672 --> 00:01:52,893
However, supply-side disinflation benefits could take years to materialize.

21
00:01:52,893 --> 00:02:00,540
Hawkish commentary from regional Fed governors reminded traders that persistent inflation could still keep interest rate hikes on the table.

22
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:05,037
This liquidity drain struck tech capital flows at the weakest point.

23
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:12,660
The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index tumbled eleven point four percent this week, shedding one point five trillion dollars.

24
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:19,694
Chip stocks have now posted eight consecutive down sessions, the longest losing streak since 2006.

25
00:02:19,694 --> 00:02:24,559
Credit default swap spreads for cloud and chip companies blew out sharply.

26
00:02:24,559 --> 00:02:32,353
Rising credit risk signals that soaring corporate borrowing costs are beginning to restrict artificial intelligence infrastructure expansion.

27
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:36,128
Wall Street remains sharply split on this tech correction.

28
00:02:36,128 --> 00:02:43,493
Barclays argues this plunge is purely a positioning unwind and mechanical leverage flush rather than fundamental AI failure.

29
00:02:43,493 --> 00:02:49,853
Jefferies sees a prime accumulation window opening up once midterm elections and the Fed meeting pass.

30
00:02:49,853 --> 00:02:59,192
Conversely, Goldman Sachs notes heavy short positioning could trigger violent short squeezes, though near-term downside volatility remains treacherous.

31
00:02:59,192 --> 00:03:07,170
Turning to our pivotal equity deep dive, CoreWeave, ticker C-R-W-V, sits at the epicenter of credit concerns.

32
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:13,064
Its five-year default probability implied by credit default swaps spiked to sixty percent.

33
00:03:13,064 --> 00:03:19,326
Borrowing premiums topped eleven percent, pushing CoreWeave effective yields above fifteen percent.

34
00:03:19,326 --> 00:03:26,998
Co-founders recently cashed out three point three billion dollars, reducing aggregate insider ownership below twenty percent.

35
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:33,358
Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang verbally defended CoreWeave operational strength in Stockholm.

36
00:03:33,358 --> 00:03:39,412
Yet, he explicitly drew the line against providing financial backstops or debt guarantees.

37
00:03:39,412 --> 00:03:46,349
Ark Invest bought aggressively into the drop, but CoreWeave plunged another twelve percent into its sixty-dollar range.

38
00:03:46,349 --> 00:03:50,540
Investors must treat this as a high-risk credit-spread proxy.

39
00:03:50,672 --> 00:03:55,732
Tesla, ticker T-S-L-A, closed lower after a short-lived gap up.

40
00:03:55,682 --> 00:04:00,780
Elon Musk unveiled the second-generation Roadster at a rocket testing facility.

41
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:06,785
The vehicle showcased cold gas thruster hops and aerodynamic ground-effect inverted driving.

42
00:04:06,785 --> 00:04:13,402
Optimus humanoid robots were positioned at the wheel, highlighting Tesla pivot toward robotics and AI.

43
00:04:13,402 --> 00:04:17,912
However, Wall Street focused squarely on execution reality.

44
00:04:17,912 --> 00:04:23,978
Production is delayed until at least 2028, offering zero near-term revenue impact.

45
00:04:23,978 --> 00:04:31,527
With third-quarter earnings scheduled for next Wednesday, analysts forecast revenue of twenty-seven point nine billion dollars.

46
00:04:31,527 --> 00:04:37,814
Compressing automotive gross margins and negative free cash flow risks could test downside support.

47
00:04:37,814 --> 00:04:42,005
Options markets imply a seven percent post-earnings swing.

48
00:04:42,005 --> 00:04:48,733
Apple, ticker A-A-P-L, provided a solitary pillar of relative strength among mega-cap tech.

49
00:04:48,733 --> 00:04:53,721
Pre-orders opened for the nineteen-hundred-and-ninety-nine dollar foldable iPhone Duo.

50
00:04:53,721 --> 00:04:58,072
High demand pushed initial delivery wait times into January.

51
00:04:58,072 --> 00:05:02,471
Counterpoint Research estimates six million unit shipments this year.

52
00:05:02,471 --> 00:05:09,897
While manufacturing yields remain a hurdle, Apple fortress balance sheet insulated the equity from broader macro selling.

53
00:05:09,897 --> 00:05:12,741
Risk management remains paramount.

54
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:18,598
Historical precedent shows late-stage panic selling is often the swiftest and most unforgiving.

55
00:05:18,598 --> 00:05:23,917
Stagger entries and avoid unhedged leverage while global catalysts digest.

56
00:05:23,917 --> 00:05:27,924
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

57
00:05:27,924 --> 00:05:32,091
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
